   They all looked up when I crossed the street. One of them said,
without lowering his voice, “Ese es el nieto.”
   “¿El de Nueva York?”
   “Mira lo flaco que está.”
   I had been in the country six hours. The whole street already knew
my weight.
   Cristian, behind the bars, was about my age, with a gold chain and a
toothpick. He got six rolls into a paper bag and a malta out of the cool‐
er and said, “Cuarenta.”
   “My grandmother said treinta.”
   He looked at me. He looked across the street at the green door. He
looked back at me, sadly, like a man watching a good deal walk out of
the room. “Treinta,” he said.
   “Siéntate, Nueva York,” said the old man standing behind the table.
He had a straw hat and a guayabera buttoned all the way to his throat,
and he pointed at an upturned crate. “Tavito,” he said, tapping his own
chest. “I knew your mother when she was this high and already had a
mouth on her.”
   I sat. Somebody handed me a plastic cup of something that was not
malta. I handed it back. Somebody handed it to me again.
   “¿Y cómo está Altagracia?” Tavito said.
   “Bien. She works too much.”
   “Siempre.” He nodded, satisfied, a man whose forecast had come
true. “She sold limoncillos on this corner when she was nine and short‐
changed me every time. Dile que Tavito todavía se acuerda.”
   Tell her Tavito still remembers.
   Tavito’s partner had a bad back and went home after the next hand,
and Tavito looked at me.
   “¿Tú juegas?”
   “A little. My tío used to—”
   “Siéntate.”
